  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "The set $A$ and its boundary $\u2202A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36},\\,\u2202A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(3,2)$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(3,0)$ is an interior point of $A$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/67d1f23f424561c69973fe5f08ad3ee3a03346f555db8f00fb71406c02a9cdf9.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,2)$ and $Q(3,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097432/assets/0b3e72b3a65241c4e03716a9c0b534d687c305a92ceef0b92d5c6df8c1b82953.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ satisfy the inequality $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\ge 36.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(3,2)$ do not satisfy the equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'.",
